All descriptive interfaces must follow the next guidelines:

1. All interface names must be prefixed by `I`.

Incorrect:

```ts
interface FooProps {}
```

Correct:

```ts
interface IFooProps {}
```

2. All "function members" MUST NOT be declared using arrows in interfaces.

Incorrect:

```ts
interface IBullet {
  shoot: (x: number, y: number) => void;
}
```

Correct:

```ts
interface IBullet {
  shoot(x: number, y: number): void;
}
```

3. Required members that are not method declarations must be placed at the top
   of the interface declaration.

Incorrect:

```ts
interface IPosition {
  z?: number;
  x: number;
  y: number;
}
```

Correct:

```ts
interface IPosition {
  x: number;
  y: number;
  z?: number;
}
```

4. Required members that are methods must be placed right below the required
   members explained on the point 1.

Incorrect:

```ts
interface IMessage {
  readMessage(): string;
  message: string;
}
```

Correct:

```ts
interface IMessage {
  message: string;
  readMessage(): string;
}
```

5. Optional members that are not method declarations must be placed at the
   bottom of the interface declaration, right below the members explained on the
   point 2.

Incorrect:

```ts
interface IUser {
  name: string;
  lastName?: string;
  setName(name: string): string;
}
```

Correct:

```ts
interface IUser {
  name: string;
  setName(name: string): string;
  lastName?: string;
}
```

6. Optional members that are methods must be placed at the end of the interface
   declaration.

Incorrect:

```ts
interface IUser {
  name: string;
  setName(name: string): string;
  setLastName?(lastName: string): string;
  lastName?: string;
}
```

Correct:

```ts
interface IUser {
  name: string;
  setName(name: string): string;
  lastName?: string;
  setLastName?(lastName: string): string;
}
```

7. If a function/method contains more than 3 parameters, then you MUST USE an
   interface as the whole arguments description. the name of this interface MUST
   BE postfixed with `Arguments`.

Incorrect:

```ts
interface ISendMail {
  from: string;
  to: string;
  message: string;
}

function sendMail(args: ISendMail) {
  /* code... */
}
```

Correct:

```ts
interface ISendMailArguments {
  from: string;
  to: string;
  message: string;
}

function sendMail(args: ISendMailArguments) {/* code... */}
```

This pattern is actually useful for functions that contains 4 or more
parameters, you MUST NOT use an interface as argument type if the function has
less than 4 arguments.

Incorrect:

```ts
interface IReconstructPathArguments {
  endNode: INode;
}

function reconstructPath(args: IReconstructPathArguments) {
  // code
}
```

Correct:

```ts
function reconstructPath(endNode: INode) {
  // code
}
```

8. If an interface is going to be used to describe the options of a function or
   method, then the name of this interface must be postfixed with `Options`.

Incorrect:

```ts
interface ISendMail {
  from: string;
}

function sendMail(to: string, message: string, options: ISendMail) {
  /* code... */
}
```

Correct:

```ts
interface ISendMailOptions {
  from: string;
}

function sendMail(to: string, message: string, options: ISendMailArguments) {
  /* code... */
}
```

9. If all the members of an interface are optional, then declare all members as
   required and use the interface wrapped on `Partial`.

Incorrect:

```ts
interface IPlayerOptions {
  lastName?: string;
  nickName?: string;
  age?: number;
}

function createPlayer(name: string, options: IPlayerOptions) {/* code... */}
```

Correct:

```ts
interface IPlayerOptions {
  lastName: string;
  nickName: string;
  age: number;
}

function createPlayer(name: string, options: Partial<IPlayerOptions>) {
  /* code... */
}
```

10. If all the members of an interface are optional, then declare all members as
    required and use the interface wrapped on `Partial`.

Incorrect:

```ts
interface IPlayerOptions {
  lastName?: string;
  nickName?: string;
  age?: number;
}

function createPlayer(name: string, options: IPlayerOptions) {
  /* code... */
}
```

Correct:

```ts
interface IPlayerOptions {
  lastName: string;
  nickName: string;
  age: number;
}

function createPlayer(name: string, options: Partial<IPlayerOptions>) {
  /* code... */
}
```

11. Always place a comment on each interface member. The comment must describe
    how the member going to work, its possible values and, if possible and
    needed: why it does exists.

Every TypeScript code must follow the next guidelines:

1. MUST NOT write "inline" returns.

Incorrect:

```ts
if (foo === 1) return;
```

Correct:

```ts
if (foo === 1) {
  return;
}
```

2. Complex condition expressions MUST BE declared on a constant before use.

Incorrect:

```ts
if (user.name === "Marcelo" && user.age > 18) {
  return;
}
```

Correct:

```ts
const isAdultMarcelo = user.name === "Marcelo" && user.age > 18;

if (isAdultMarcelo) {
  return;
}
```

Better:

This approach allows easy debugging.

```ts
const isUserAdult = user.age > 18;
const isUserNameMarcelo = user.name === "Marcelo";
const isAdultMarcelo = isUserAdult && isUserNameMarcelo;

if (isAdultMarcelo) {
  return;
}
```

3. The result expression of a function or method MUST BE stored in a constant or
   local variable before return it.

Incorrect:

```ts
function isEven(n: number) {
  return n % 2 === 0;
}
```

Correct:

```ts
function isEven(n: number) {
  const isEven = n % 2 === 0;

  return isEven;
}
```

4. Simple return expressions MUST NOT BE stored in a constant or local variable
   before return it.

Incorrect:

```ts
function oneOnly() {
  const result = 1;

  return result;
}
```

```ts
enum UserType {
  ADMIN = "admin",
  USER = "user",
}

function adminType() {
  const result = UserType.ADMIN;

  return result;
}
```

Correct:

```ts
function oneOnly() {
  return 1;
}
```

```ts
enum UserType {
  ADMIN = "admin",
  USER = "user",
}

function adminType() {
  return UserType.ADMIN;
}
```

If the possible result of a function or method is already a variable or
constant, then DO NOT store it on a new constant or variable.

Incorrect:

```ts
function bubbleSort(values: number[]) {
  const numbers = [...values];
  const numbersLength = numbers.length;
  const isTrivialLength = numbersLength <= 1;

  if (isTrivialLength) {
    const sortedNumbers = numbers; // < WRONG!

    return sortedNumbers;
  }

  // Code...
}
```

Correct:

```ts
function bubbleSort(values: number[]) {
  const numbers = [...values];
  const numbersLength = numbers.length;
  const isTrivialLength = numbersLength <= 1;

  if (isTrivialLength) {
    return numbers; // < CORRECT!
  }

  // Code...
}
```

Better:

```ts
function bubbleSort(values: number[]) {
  const numbersLength = values.length;
  const isTrivialLength = numbersLength <= 1;

  if (isTrivialLength) {
    return values; // < CORRECT!
  }

  const numbers = [...values];

  // Code...
}
```

5. MUST place an space before first-level return's.

Incorrect:

```ts
function isOdd(value: number) {
  const isOdd = value % 2 === 1;
  return isOdd;
}
```

Correct:

```ts
function isOdd(value: number) {
  const isOdd = value % 2 === 1;

  return isOdd;
}
```

6. USE switch true when possible.

Incorrect:

```ts
if (user.age >= 18) {
  return "You're an adult";
} else if (user.age >= 12) {
  return "You're a teenanger";
} else if (user.age >= 5) {
  return "You're a kid";
}

return "You're a baby";
```

Correct:

```ts
switch (true) {
  case user.age >= 18:
    return "You're an adult";
  case user.age >= 12:
    return "You're an teenanger";
  case user.age >= 5:
    return "You're an kid";
  default:
    return "You're a baby";
}
```

7. Avoid multiple assignation to a `let` variable when possible.

Incorrect:

```ts
let ageText = "";
const isPlusEqual18 = movie.acceptableAge >= 18;
const isPlusEqual12 = movie.acceptableAge >= 12;

if (isPlusEqual18) {
  ageText = "+=18";
} else if (isPlusEqual12) {
  ageText = "+=12";
} else {
  ageText = "<12";
}

console.log({ ageText });
```

Correct:

```ts
const ageText = (() => {
  const isPlusEqual18 = movie.acceptableAge >= 18;
  const isPlusEqual12 = movie.acceptableAge >= 12;

  switch (true) {
    case isPlusEqual18:
      return "+=18";
    case isPlusEqual12:
      return "+=12";
    default:
      return "<12";
  }
})();

console.log({ ageText });
```

8. Avoid typing variable/constant declarations as much as possible.

Incorrect:

```ts
const ageText: string = "";
const name: string = "Alexander";
let res: Promise<IUser> = foo();
```

Correct:

```ts
const ageText = "";
const name = "Alexander";
let res: = foo(); // Self infered.
```

9. Avoid writting comments unless the logic is too much complex for a medium
   level developer.
10. The naming, in general, must be descriptive and explicit.

Incorrect:

In this case, there is not context to explain where `tables` come from.

```ts
const tables = [] as ITable[];
const userInTable = tables.some((table) =>
  table.participants.includes(user.id)
);
```

Correct:

```ts
const restaurantTables = [] as ITable[];
const isUserPresentInSomeTable = tables.some((restaurantTable) =>
  restaurantTable.participants.includes(user.id)
);
```

Incorrect:

If the context of the declaration is explicit, like the following one, then you
can use non-explicit variable names.

```ts
function createRestaurant(name: string) {
  const restaurantTables = [] as ITable[];

  return { restaurantTables };
}

const restaurant = createRestaurant("My Super Restaurant");

console.log(restaurant.restaurantTables); // Ugly!
```

Correct:

```ts
function createRestaurant(name: string) {
  const tables = [] as ITable[];

  return { tables };
}

const restaurant = createRestaurant("My Super Restaurant");

console.log(restaurant.tables); // Nice!
```

11. Boolean variables and constant must start with `is`, `has`, `should`,
    `could`, `may`, `must`, `can`, or `are`. Negative prefixes are also allowed:
    `isNot`, `hasNot`, `shouldNot`, `couldNot`, `mayNot`, `mustNot`, `canNot`,
    or `areNot`

Incorrect:

```ts
const tablesWithSpace = tables.some((table) => table.participants.length === 0);
const userIsAdult = user.age >= 18;
const carWorking = car.gasLevel >= 0.5;
```

Correct:

```ts
const areTablesAvailable = tables.some((table) =>
  table.participants.length === 0
);
const isTheUserAnAdult = user.age >= 18;
const isNotUserAnAdult = user.age < 18;
const isCarWorking = car.gasLevel >= 0.5;
```

12. Always place an `[BY-AI]` tag before any comment generated by you outside
    the types.
13. Always place a description of the reason you're using `@ts-ignore`.

Incorrect:

```ts
async function foo() {
  return 1;
}

// @ts-ignore
const wrongCalc = 2 + foo(); // Error due we can't add 2 to a promise.
```

Correct:

```ts
async function foo() {
  return 1;
}

// @ts-ignore: [BY-AI] I placed `@ts-ignore` because i'm dumb and I didn't remember I can't add 2 to apromise... then it does not work and this `@ts-ignore` is wrong placed.
const wrongCalc = 2 + foo(); // Error due we can't add 2 to a promise.
```

14. Avoid using unions as much as possible, do use enums instead.
15. Always destructure objects inside the code block.

Incorrect:

```ts
interface IFooArguments {
  a: number;
  b: number;
}

async function foo({ a, b }: IFooArguments) {
  const result = a + b;

  return result;
}
```

Correct:

```ts
interface IFooArguments {
  a: number;
  b: number;
}

async function foo(args: IFooArguments) {
  const { a, b } = args;
  const result = a + b;

  return result;
}
```

16. Do not repeat yourself.
17. Keep your code simple.
18. Code that will be reused along the project must be placed on a separate file
    to be recicled.
19. Do never create types on function arguments.

Incorrect:

```ts
async function sum(args: { a: number; b: number }) {
  const { a, b } = args;
  const result = a + b;

  return result;
}
```

Correct:

```ts
interface ISumArguments {
  a: number;
  b: number;
}

async function sum(args: IFooArguments) {
  const { a, b } = args;
  const result = a + b;

  return result;
}
```

20. If possible, pass a function directly as argument instead of create an arrow
    function.

Incorrect:

```ts
async function foo() {
  throw new Error("New error found!");
}

foo().catch((error) => console.error(error));
```

Correct:

```ts
async function foo() {
  throw new Error("New error found!");
}

foo().catch(console.error);
```

21. Avoid unhandled promises.

Incorrect:

```ts
async function foo() {
  throw new Error("New error found!");
}

foo();
```

Correct:

```ts
async function foo() {
  throw new Error("New error found!");
}

await foo();
```

22. Use `Promise.all` when operations are independent.

Incorrect:

```ts
await fakeRequest1();
await fakeRequest2();
await fakeRequest3();
```

Correct:

```ts
await Promise.all([
  fakeRequest1(),
  fakeRequest2(),
  fakeRequest3(),
]);
```

23. Constant declarations must be separed from the expressions.

Incorrect:

```ts
if (isFirstNumberGreater) {
  const numberAtIndex = numbers[innerIndex];
  numbers[innerIndex] = numbers[innerIndex + 1];
  numbers[innerIndex + 1] = numberAtIndex;
}
```

Correct:

```ts
if (isFirstNumberGreater) {
  const numberAtIndex = numbers[innerIndex];

  numbers[innerIndex] = numbers[innerIndex + 1];
  numbers[innerIndex + 1] = numberAtIndex;
}
```

24. Exports must be inlined.

25. Make sure you're using your declarations.

Incorrect:

```ts
const firstNumber = numbers[innerIndex]; // NOT BEING COMPLETLY USED
const secondNumber = numbers[innerIndex + 1]; // NOT BEING COMPLETLY USED
const isFirstNumberGreater = firstNumber > secondNumber;

if (isFirstNumberGreater) {
  const numberAtIndex = numbers[innerIndex]; // < USELESS AND REDUNDANT DECLARATION

  numbers[innerIndex] = numbers[innerIndex + 1];
  numbers[innerIndex + 1] = numberAtIndex;
}
```

Correct:

```ts
const firstNumber = numbers[innerIndex]; // < COMPLETLY USED
const secondNumber = numbers[innerIndex + 1]; // < COMPLETLY USED
const isFirstNumberGreater = firstNumber > secondNumber;

if (isFirstNumberGreater) {
  numbers[innerIndex] = secondNumber;
  numbers[innerIndex + 1] = firstNumber;
}
```
